  <h3 id="listen">The Go listens to the channel before transmitting.</h3>
  <p class="lede">ADS-L requires listen-before-talk. This follows the polite spectrum access rules of ETSI EN 300 220-1. Before the scheduled instant, the Go measures received power on the channel for 200&nbsp;&micro;s. It averages nine readings as power. The measurement ends no more than 2&nbsp;ms before transmission. If the power is below the threshold, it transmits. At or above the threshold, it waits, then listens again. The wait is chosen at random between 15 and 250&nbsp;ms in steps of 5&nbsp;ms. Each step is one booked burst duration. The 15 to 250&nbsp;ms backoff range comes from ADS-L 4 SRD-860 C.2. The Go considers only waits that still allow a permitted start within the window. If no wait of at least 15&nbsp;ms fits, the burst is missed. The wait counts from the burst&rsquo;s scheduled instant. The address and that instant determine the draw.</p>
  <p class="lede">The threshold comes from the standard&rsquo;s rule for a 200&nbsp;kHz channel, adjusted for antenna gain. At the radio chip, the channel counts as busy from &minus;78&nbsp;dBm. The antenna gain and feed loss used in this calculation come from the datasheet and an allowance, not from measurements on a unit.</p>
  <p class="lede">A deferred burst stays in its window and on its channel. For a position, the Go prepares the message again for the new start time, using the newest GPS fix. If the channel stays busy until the window closes, the burst is missed. On the ground, once 3&nbsp;s have passed since its first busy assessment, the Go may force a transmission without listening, as ADS-L permits. It then stays silent for 2&nbsp;s after the burst ends. It never forces a transmission in flight, where that silence would cost two positions.</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>EN 300 220-1 parameter</th><th>Declared</th><th>The standard allows</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Minimum channel measurement</th><td>200&nbsp;&micro;s</td><td>at least 160&nbsp;&micro;s</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Maximum gap between measurement and transmission</th><td>2&nbsp;ms</td><td>at most 5&nbsp;ms</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Method used to randomise a deferral</th><td>a hash of the address and the burst&rsquo;s scheduled instant</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Deferral step</th><td>5&nbsp;ms, one booked burst duration</td><td>at least the measurement duration</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Minimum and maximum deferral</th><td>15 and 250&nbsp;ms</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Maximum transmission duration</th><td>5&nbsp;ms</td><td>at most 1&nbsp;s</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Minimum silence on the same frequency</th><td>100&nbsp;ms</td><td>at least 100&nbsp;ms</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">Listening cannot prevent every collision. It can detect only a burst already on air, and it measures the channel at the Go&rsquo;s antenna, not at the receiver. Another aircraft may hear two overlapping bursts even when neither transmitter hears the other. The check also favours nearby, stronger signals over distant ones. The timing design therefore relies on a fresh draw every second and the 1&nbsp;% duty-cycle limit, rather than on listening alone.</p>

  <h3 id="retry">The Go can retry a missed burst 2&nbsp;s later.</h3>
  <p class="lede">A burst can miss its scheduled second because the GPS fix is too old, the channel stays busy or the permitted transmission time for the hour is exhausted. Another attempt is possible 2&nbsp;s later. The second is then still even or still odd, which keeps the transmission on the same channel. If that channel&rsquo;s window already carries one of the unit&rsquo;s bursts, the retry waits for the next free second of the same parity. It gets opportunities every 2&nbsp;s until it succeeds or the next message of its type is due. At that point, the Go gives up on the old one and counts the miss. On the ground, a position missed at c can be retried at c + 2, c + 4, c + 6 and c + 8. In flight, positions are not retried: the new position 2&nbsp;s later already uses the same channel.</p>

  <h3 id="callsign">The callsign goes at the end of window 2.</h3>
  <p class="lede">ADS-L Issue 2 has no callsign field. The Go sends its callsign in OGN-Tracker&rsquo;s record format, with a start between 1000 and 1190&nbsp;ms on 868.4&nbsp;MHz. This is the end of window 2, outside the direct slot reserved for ADS-L messages and where ADS-L transmits nothing today. <%= link_to_page "ads-l/callsign", label: "The callsign question" %> explains that choice, and what changes once Issue 3 defines a field.</p>
  <p class="lede">OGN-Tracker ignores a callsign if it knows no target with that address. The position therefore needs to arrive before the callsign. With the normal schedule in flight, that position is at most a second old. On the ground, the callsign is sent 4 or 5&nbsp;s after a position, well before a receiver forgets a ground target between its reports.</p>

  <h3 id="budget">The Go limits transmission to 1&nbsp;% of any hour.</h3>
  <p class="lede">The Go declares a limit of 1&nbsp;% of any hour on air for its use of the 868&nbsp;MHz band. It keeps sixty one-minute counters and checks the budget before every burst. A position every second plus a Status and a callsign every 10&nbsp;s uses 6&nbsp;ms per second: 0.6&nbsp;% of the time in flight, or 0.15&nbsp;% on the ground. The normal schedule cannot exhaust the budget. If a fault does exhaust it, the Go blocks transmission rather than keep occupying the band.</p>

  <h3 id="clock">GPS sets the clock, and the clock gates the radio.</h3>
  <p class="lede">The GPS receiver sends a pulse per second, called PPS. It provides the reference for the radio windows. To transmit, Go needs an activated address and a radio that answers and listens before transmission. The GPS receiver must calculate a position on each second and half-second and send its data at 115200 baud. Go also needs a <%= link_to_page "gps", label: "settled fix", anchor: "settle" %>, valid UTC time and a pulse for the current second. These two GPS positions per second keep every transmitted position under ADS-L&rsquo;s 500&nbsp;ms age limit. If a requirement fails, Go stops transmitting. If a pulse is missing, transmission stops 1.2&nbsp;s after the last pulse. The radio windows continue for 60&nbsp;s from that last pulse, for reception only. After 10&nbsp;s without meeting a requirement, every page shows <span class="mono">NO TX</span> and the reason (<%= link_to_page "radar", label: "When Go does not transmit", anchor: "no-tx" %>).</p>
  <p class="lede">The GPS receiver reports the UTC second in a data sentence that arrives hundreds of milliseconds after the pulse. While waiting for that sentence, Go still has the report for the previous second. Using it to timestamp a received burst could assign the burst to the wrong second. Two Go units exposed the problem on the bench: a burst read 42:52.954 on the sender and 42:53.956 on the receiver. Received bursts are now dated from the UTC second opened by the latest pulse.</p>
  <p class="lede">The firmware&rsquo;s service loop runs every 10&nbsp;ms, too slowly to open each radio window on time itself. Early bench captures showed window 2 opening 3 to 6&nbsp;ms late at the median, and up to 120&nbsp;ms late at worst. The firmware now gives the radio its next window as soon as the current one starts, with an absolute deadline calculated from the pulse. The radio can then switch without waiting for another pass through the loop.</p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>The serial&rsquo;s last digit sets the start of the 20&nbsp;s cycle</li>
    <li>An even second puts the position on 868.2&nbsp;MHz, an odd one on 868.4&nbsp;MHz</li>
    <li>On the ground, the Go schedules two positions, two Status messages and two callsigns every 20&nbsp;s</li>
    <li>In flight, it schedules a position every second without moving the Status or callsign schedule</li>
    <li>It draws the instant within the window again every second</li>
    <li>It checks the channel before normal transmissions and gives missed bursts another opportunity 2&nbsp;s later, except for positions in flight</li>
  </ul>
</section>

